I’m sure everyone has heard about AI. Nowadays, almost everyone is familiar with it, so the basics are already known to most people. But what exactly is AI?
AI stands for Artificial Intelligence. Here, artificial means something that is created by humans, and intelligence refers to the ability to think, learn, understand, and make decisions. In simple terms, AI is a human-made technology that is designed to mimic human intelligence
Today's AI is much more advanced than before. In fact, some people even say that AI can perform certain tasks better and faster than humans. That is why modern AI is often compared to human intelligence, and in the future, it may become even more powerful.
If we look at older machines, they worked based on fixed programs. We gave them instructions, commands, and predefined techniques, and they produced results accordingly. They could only do what they were programmed to do.
However, modern AI is different. Instead of only following fixed instructions, AI can learn from data. It can identify patterns, gain insights, and improve its performance over time. It can work with different types of data such as text, images, speech, and videos.
Examples of modern AI include self-driving cars, speech recognition systems, recommendation systems, and virtual assistants. What makes AI special is its ability to learn from data, recognize patterns, and solve complex problems effectively.

==============================Types of AI=================================================================================


Now let's talk about the types of AI. AI can be categorized into different types based on its capabilities and the way it performs tasks. Understanding these types helps us better understand how AI works.

Reactive Machine AI

The first type is Reactive Machine AI. This is the most basic form of AI. These systems do not have memory and cannot learn from past experiences. They simply react to the current situation and make decisions based on the data available at that moment.
Since they have no memory, they cannot improve themselves by learning from previous actions. They only perform the tasks for which they have been programmed.
