One advantage of Reactive Machine AI is that it is relatively simple to design and can perform specific tasks very efficiently. However, its biggest limitation is that it cannot learn, adapt, or understand situations beyond its programming. Because of this, it cannot handle complex or changing environments effectively

Limited Memory AI

The second type of AI is called Limited Memory AI.
As the name suggests, Limited Memory AI can learn from past data and experiences for a certain period of time. Unlike Reactive Machines, these systems can store information, analyze previous data, and use it to make better decisions in the future.
Most modern AI applications fall into this category. These systems are trained on large amounts of data, learn patterns from that data, and continuously improve their performance over time.
Some common examples of Limited Memory AI include:
•	ChatGPT
•	Microsoft Copilot
•	Self-driving cars
•	Netflix recommendation systems
•	YouTube recommendations
•	Amazon product recommendations
•	Virtual assistants

These systems learn from user interactions, historical data, and feedback to provide more accurate and personalized results.
Advantages

•	Can learn from historical data.
•	Improves performance over time.
•	More accurate and efficient than Reactive Machines.
•	Useful for real-world applications such as recommendations, automation, and decision-making.

In short, Limited Memory AI is the most common form of AI today. It learns from past information, recognizes patterns, and makes smarter decisions, which is why technologies like ChatGPT, Microsoft Copilot, Netflix, and self-driving cars are possible.

Theory of Mind AI

Now let's talk about what AI may be capable of in the future.
The next type of AI is called Theory of Mind AI. This is still a research concept, and no true Theory of Mind AI exists today.
The idea behind this AI is that it would be able to understand human emotions, beliefs, intentions, and behavior. It would not only process data but also recognize how people feel and respond accordingly.
For example, if a person is happy, sad, stressed, or frustrated, a Theory of Mind AI would be able to identify those emotions and adjust its responses or actions. It could understand human moods, social interactions, and emotional contexts much better than current AI systems.
At present, researchers are actively working in this area, but AI has not yet reached this level of understanding. Today's AI can recognize patterns in data, but it does not truly understand human emotions or intentions the way humans do.

Self-Aware AI

The most advanced and theoretical form of AI is Self-Aware AI.
Self-Aware AI refers to a system that would have consciousness and self-awareness. In other words, it would be aware of its own existence, understand its internal state, and potentially make decisions based on its own thoughts and understanding.
This type of AI remains purely hypothetical. No such AI exists today, and scientists are still debating whether it will ever be possible.
If Self-Aware AI were developed in the future, it could possess abilities far beyond current AI systems. However, this also raises important ethical, safety, and societal questions.
For now, Self-Aware AI belongs to the realm of future possibilities rather than current reality.


=====================================AI Types Based on Capabilities=====================================

Now that we have discussed the types of AI, let's look at another important classification of AI based on its capabilities.
ANI – Artificial Narrow Intelligence
ANI stands for Artificial Narrow Intelligence. This is the type of AI that exists today.
ANI is designed to perform specific tasks and can become highly efficient in those areas. However, it cannot perform tasks outside its designated domain.
Examples include:
•	Google Search
•	Microsoft Copilot
•	Siri
•	Alexa
•	Recommendation systems used by Netflix, YouTube, and Amazon
Although ANI can perform some tasks better than humans, it does not possess general human intelligence.

AGI – Artificial General Intelligence
AGI stands for Artificial General Intelligence.
AGI refers to an AI system that can think, learn, reason, and solve problems across different domains, similar to a human being. Unlike ANI, AGI would not be limited to a single task.
An AGI system would be able to:
•	Learn new skills independently
•	Understand and apply knowledge across multiple fields
•	Reason and make decisions like humans
•	Adapt to unfamiliar situations
Currently, AGI does not exist and remains a major goal of AI research.

ASI – Artificial Super Intelligence
ASI stands for Artificial Super Intelligence.
This is a hypothetical future form of AI that would surpass human intelligence in almost every aspect, including learning, reasoning, creativity, and decision-making.
ASI could potentially:
•	Solve highly complex problems
•	Make discoveries beyond human capabilities
•	Learn and improve itself rapidly
•	Outperform humans in virtually all intellectual tasks
However, ASI remains a theoretical concept and has not been developed.

AI Types by Capability:
ANI (Artificial Narrow Intelligence) – Exists today
AGI (Artificial General Intelligence) – Future goal
ASI (Artificial Super Intelligence) – Theoretical future concept
Today, systems such as ChatGPT, Microsoft Copilot, recommendation engines, virtual assistants, and autonomous vehicles belong primarily to the ANI category and mostly use Limited Memory AI techniques. AGI and ASI are still subjects of ongoing research and future development.
